---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Calculator from '../components/Calculator';
---

<Base
  title="Shipping Label Printing Cost Calculator & Setup Comparison"
  description="Estimate thermal label cost per usable label, monthly and annual costs, waste and printer allocation. Compare two setups with your own quotes. Postage excluded."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span>Printing cost calculator</span>
      </nav>
      <h1>
        Shipping label costs.
        <br />
        <em>Beyond the price tag.</em>
      </h1>
      <p>
        How much will printing shipping labels actually cost your business? Explore volume,
        compatible stock and printer investment, then compare two setups with your own quotes.
        Printing costs only. Postage is separate.
      </p>
    </header>
    <Calculator client:load />
    <noscript>
      <p>
        Enable JavaScript to update the estimate. The{' '}
        <a href="/guides/printing-costs/">printing cost guide</a> explains the formula.
      </p>
    </noscript>
    <section class="cost-explainer" aria-label="Formulas and frequently asked questions">
      <div>
        <h2>Know what is in your estimate.</h2>
        <p>
          This browser-based tool models a representative month in USD. Profiles are editable
          arithmetic examples, not measured business averages or current product offers.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Usable labels:</strong> daily usable labels × operating days. One usable label
            per parcel.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Consumed stock:</strong> round up usable labels ÷ (1 − waste % ÷ 100). Waste is
            a share of consumed stock, not a markup on orders.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Consumables:</strong> consumed labels × (pack price ÷ labels per pack). Waste
            cost is already included, never added twice.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Monthly estimate:</strong> consumables + optional monthly costs + purchase price
            ÷ estimated useful life in months.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Annualized estimate:</strong> monthly estimate × 12. This allocation view
            differs from the comparison table’s upfront purchase + operating costs.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <h3>A worked example</h3>
        <p>
          Illustrative inputs: 50 usable labels per day, 20 days, 20% waste, a $20 pack of 500, and
          a $240 printer allocated over 24 months. You consume 1,250 labels: $50 of stock, including
          $10 of waste. With no optional costs, the monthly estimate is $60, or $0.06 per usable
          label. Annualized: $720. Buying three whole packs needs $60 of stock cash, with labels
          left over; consumption cost is a different figure.
        </p>
        <p>
          Calculations retain precision until display; label counts round upward. Taxes, postage,
          financing, staff time and automatic printer replacement are excluded. Optional costs start
          at zero. Prices, volume and waste are assumed constant.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Before you compare.</h2>
        <details>
          <summary>Does this include shipping postage?</summary>
          <p>
            No. This estimates the cost to print a label, not the carrier’s charge to transport a
            package. Use carrier or platform quotes for postage.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why divide by the labels that are not wasted?</summary>
          <p>
            If 20% of consumed labels are lost, only 80% become usable. To produce 1,000 usable
            labels, you need 1,000 ÷ 0.8 = 1,250 consumed labels. Waste is already part of that
            stock cost.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is annualized cost my first-year cash expense?</summary>
          <p>
            No. The main estimate allocates hardware over your estimated useful life. The scenario
            table shows the purchase once plus operating costs. Whole-pack purchases can need more
            cash than proportional consumption. Neither view automatically replaces a printer.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How should I estimate electricity?</summary>
          <p>
            Use measured monthly kWh or a documented estimate and your actual tariff. Rated power
            alone is not monthly consumption. Leave both fields at zero when you lack sufficient
            data.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can all printers use the same label quote?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Our Rollo Wireless and MUNBYN ITPP941 USB entries use generic direct-thermal stock;
            Brother QL-1110NWB uses DK-format media. Verify dimensions, media and printer
            requirements. See the <a href="/guides/printing-costs/">sourced cost guide</a> and{' '}
            <a href="/guides/etsy-4x6-label-size/">4 × 6 label guide</a>.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are shared estimates private?</summary>
          <p>
            The calculator submits nothing to a Tagrivo server. A share link contains numeric
            assumptions in its URL fragment; anyone receiving it can read those numbers. Our
            analytics events omit calculator answers and quotes. Clipboard and download actions
            require your click.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does the cheapest estimate identify the best printer?</summary>
          <p>
            No. The model does not measure compatibility, support, durability, speed or reliability.
            Use the <a href="/printer-finder/">Printer Finder</a> and{' '}
            <a href="/compare/">specification comparison</a> before purchasing.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
